--TEST--
`Core\Test`'s failure messages name an `array<T>` and a `bytes` by their size and an object by its class, so no container's contents are ever quoted -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring bound on a quoted `string` would buy nothing if a container
// spilled its elements instead, so `shown` renders every container as a size
// and every object as a class name. That is the same reason -- a message goes
// to a build log -- stated for the shapes that have no length of their own,
// and it is why `assertEqualsDeep` says *where* two subjects differ: the leaf
// it names is the only thing a reader wants quoted.

final class Cell {
    public function constructor(public string $name) {}
}

int $refused = 0;

// Every element here is 70 characters, past the quoting bound next door. None
// of them appears: what is printed is a count, and the two subjects differ by
// their length alone.
string $long = Core\Str::repeat("x", 70);
array<string> $two = [$long, $long];
array<string> $one = [$long];
try { Core\Test::assertSame($two, $one); } catch (Throwable $sized) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $sized->message, "\n"; }

// Nesting changes nothing, and an empty container is still named by its size
// rather than by a word of its own -- a rendering that walked one level to
// decide what to say would print differently on exactly these two rows.
array<array<string>> $deep = [[$long], [$long], [$long]];
array<array<string>> $none = [];
try { Core\Test::assertSame($deep, $none); } catch (Throwable $nested) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $nested->message, "\n"; }

// Two objects of one class, holding different property values, render
// identically: the class name is the whole of what is said. This is the row a
// rendering that reached for a property would fail, and it is why the message
// below repeats itself.
Cell $held = new Cell($long);
Cell $other = new Cell("-");
try { Core\Test::assertSame($held, $other); } catch (Throwable $classed) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $classed->message, "\n"; }

// `bytes` is the third: a count, never a decoded run, because `rule:types/bytes` makes
// it the type with no promise of being text at all.
bytes $wide = $long as bytes;
bytes $thin = "-" as bytes;
try { Core\Test::assertSame($wide, $thin); } catch (Throwable $counted) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $counted->message, "\n"; }

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: a member that started
// answering one of these instead of refusing fails here.
echo "refused=", $refused, "/4\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is an array of 2, `$expected` is an array of 1
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is an array of 3, `$expected` is an array of 0
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is a `Cell`, `$expected` is a `Cell`
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 70 bytes, `$expected` is 1 bytes
refused=4/4
